A common shortcut is using one DTO for both request and response. This creates a different coupling problem: the fields a client sends to create a resource are rarely the same fields they need in the response.

A `CreateUserRequest` has `name`, `email`, `password`. A `UserResponse` has `id`, `name`, `email`, `createdAt` — no password, and includes server-generated fields the client never sends. Using one class means either accepting fields you shouldn't (`id` in a create request) or annotating half the fields with `@JsonIgnore` depending on direction.

```java
public record CreateUserRequest(
    @NotBlank String name,
    @Email String email,
    @Size(min = 8) String password
) {}

public record UserResponse(
    Long id,
    String name,
    String email,
    LocalDateTime createdAt
) {}
```

Java `record` classes are ideal for DTOs — they're immutable, generate `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString`, and the constructor enforces that all fields are provided. Validation annotations (`@NotBlank`, `@Email`) go on request DTOs only, keeping response classes clean.

For mapping between entities and DTOs, **MapStruct** generates type-safe mapping code at compile time — no reflection, no runtime overhead. A single interface with `@Mapper` produces the implementation automatically. This eliminates the boilerplate objection that makes developers skip DTOs in the first place.

One pattern worth knowing: **projection DTOs** for list endpoints. A `UserListItem` with just `id`, `name`, and `role` is lighter than the full `UserResponse` with all fields. Spring Data JPA even supports interface-based projections that generate the query to select only the needed columns, reducing both network payload and database load.

The conversion between entities and DTOs needs a home. Putting it in the controller clutters request handling. Putting it in the service mixes business logic with serialization concerns. The cleanest approach is a dedicated **mapper layer**.

MapStruct is the standard choice for Java projects. You define a mapper interface, annotate it with `@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")`, and MapStruct generates the implementation at compile time.

```java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface UserMapper {
    UserResponse toResponse(User entity);
    User toEntity(CreateUserRequest request);

    @Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "createdAt", ignore = true)
    void updateEntity(UpdateUserRequest request, @MappingTarget User entity);
}
```

The `@MappingTarget` annotation handles update operations — MapStruct applies only the fields from the request to the existing entity, preserving server-managed fields like `id` and `createdAt`. This eliminates the manual field-by-field copy that's both tedious and error-prone.

The generated code is plain Java method calls — no reflection, no proxy magic, full IDE debuggability. This matters when a mapping bug surfaces in production: you can step through the generated source instead of debugging a runtime framework.

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/v1/orders")                         // plural, prefix-versioned
class OrderController {

    private final OrderService orders;
    private final OrderItemService items;

    // GET /v1/orders?status=paid&createdAfter=...&sort=-createdAt&cursor=...
    @GetMapping
    Page<OrderDto> list(OrderFilter filter, Pageable page) {
        return orders.list(filter, page);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    OrderDto get(@PathVariable String id) { return orders.get(id); }

    @PostMapping
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    OrderDto create(@RequestBody @Valid CreateOrderRequest req) {
        return orders.create(req);
    }

    @PutMapping("/{id}")                              // full replacement
    OrderDto replace(@PathVariable String id,
                     @RequestBody @Valid ReplaceOrderRequest req) {
        return orders.replace(id, req);
    }

    @PatchMapping("/{id}")                            // partial update
    OrderDto update(@PathVariable String id,
                    @RequestBody @Valid UpdateOrderRequest req) {
        return orders.update(id, req);
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
    void delete(@PathVariable String id) { orders.delete(id); }

    // --- Sub-resource: items that belong to an order ---
    @GetMapping("/{id}/items")
    List<OrderItemDto> listItems(@PathVariable("id") String orderId) {
        return items.listForOrder(orderId);
    }

    @PostMapping("/{id}/items")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    OrderItemDto addItem(@PathVariable("id") String orderId,
                         @RequestBody @Valid AddItemRequest req) {
        return items.add(orderId, req);
    }

    // --- Controller-style action (Stripe convention) ---
    @PostMapping("/{id}:cancel")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED)
    OrderDto cancel(@PathVariable String id, @RequestBody CancelReason reason) {
        return orders.cancel(id, reason);
    }
}

// Domain exceptions carry a stable code + status
public abstract class ApiException extends RuntimeException {
    private final HttpStatus status;
    private final String code;
    protected ApiException(HttpStatus status, String code, String message) {
        super(message); this.status = status; this.code = code;
    }
    public HttpStatus status() { return status; }
    public String code()       { return code; }
}

public class InsufficientFundsException extends ApiException {
    private final BigDecimal balance, required;
    public InsufficientFundsException(BigDecimal balance, BigDecimal required) {
        super(HttpStatus.PAYMENT_REQUIRED, "BILLING_INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS",
              "Balance %s, required %s".formatted(balance, required));
        this.balance = balance; this.required = required;
    }
    public BigDecimal balance()  { return balance; }
    public BigDecimal required() { return required; }
}

// Single advice translates every exception into ProblemDetail
@RestControllerAdvice
class GlobalErrorHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {

    // Domain exceptions
    @ExceptionHandler(ApiException.class)
    ProblemDetail handleApi(ApiException ex, HttpServletRequest req) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(ex.status(), ex.getMessage());
        pd.setType(URI.create("https://errors.api.acme.com/" + ex.code().toLowerCase()
                                                                     .replace('_', '/')));
        pd.setTitle(defaultTitleFor(ex.status()));
        pd.setInstance(URI.create(req.getRequestURI()));
        pd.setProperty("code", ex.code());
        pd.setProperty("traceId", MDC.get("traceId"));
        enrichExtensions(pd, ex);
        return pd;
    }

    // @Valid bean-validation errors
    @Override
    protected ResponseEntity<Object> handleMethodArgumentNotValid(
            MethodArgumentNotValidException ex, HttpHeaders h, HttpStatusCode st, WebRequest req) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST,
                "%d field(s) failed validation.".formatted(ex.getFieldErrorCount()));
        pd.setType(URI.create("https://errors.api.acme.com/validation/invalid-request"));
        pd.setTitle("Validation failed");
        pd.setProperty("code", "VALIDATION_FAILED");
        pd.setProperty("traceId", MDC.get("traceId"));
        pd.setProperty("errors", ex.getFieldErrors().stream()
            .map(fe -> Map.of(
                "field",   fe.getField(),
                "code",    Optional.ofNullable(fe.getCode()).orElse("INVALID"),
                "message", fe.getDefaultMessage()))
            .toList());
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
            .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_PROBLEM_JSON).body(pd);
    }

    // Anything unhandled — never leak the stack trace
    @ExceptionHandler(Throwable.class)
    ProblemDetail handleUnexpected(Throwable ex, HttpServletRequest req) {
        log.error("unexpected", ex);      // full detail stays in logs
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(
            HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
            "An unexpected error occurred. Reference the traceId to contact support.");
        pd.setType(URI.create("https://errors.api.acme.com/system/internal"));
        pd.setTitle("Internal server error");
        pd.setProperty("code", "INTERNAL_ERROR");
        pd.setProperty("traceId", MDC.get("traceId"));
        return pd;
    }

    private void enrichExtensions(ProblemDetail pd, ApiException ex) {
        if (ex instanceof InsufficientFundsException ins) {
            pd.setProperty("balance",  ins.balance().toPlainString());
            pd.setProperty("required", ins.required().toPlainString());
        }
        // add more ex-type enrichments here
    }
}

# Single-error shape, nested object
HTTP/1.1 402 Payment Required
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "error": {
    "type":    "card_error",
    "code":    "insufficient_funds",
    "message": "Your card has insufficient funds.",
    "param":   "card",
    "docUrl":  "https://stripe.com/docs/error-codes#insufficient-funds",
    "requestId": "req_7a9c3f12"
  }
}

# Validation with per-field detail
HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "error": {
    "type":    "invalid_request_error",
    "code":    "parameter_invalid",
    "message": "2 parameters invalid",
    "errors": [
      { "param": "email",     "code": "not_email",   "message": "invalid email" },
      { "param": "birthDate", "code": "in_past",     "message": "must be in the past" }
    ]
  }
}

# The rule regardless of shape:
#  - one envelope everywhere
#  - stable machine-readable `code`
#  - correlation id for support
#  - no stack traces, no internal paths, no SQL messages
